# Line-ending pins for byte-compared artifacts.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, so a text
# file committed as LF is checked out as CRLF. Any test that re-derives an
# artifact and byte-compares it against the committed copy therefore goes red on
# a fresh clone for a file nobody changed: the rebuild writes LF, the checkout
# holds CRLF.
#
# PRD Phase 2 exit criterion 2 requires calibration recomputation to be
# "byte-identical on re-run", and the Phase 1 ground-truth CSVs are the hashed
# inputs that feed it. Both are pinned to LF so the comparison measures the
# pipeline rather than the checkout.

# Phase 2 (FR-2.2): deterministic calibration artifacts and their inputs.
data/calibration/*.json          text eol=lf
data/forecast_archive/*.csv      text eol=lf

# Phase 1 (FR-1.6): immutable CLI ground-truth record.
data/weather_truth/*.csv         text eol=lf

# Phase 2 (FR-2.4): recorded ladder history replayed by the go/no-go report.
data/ladders/**                  text eol=lf

# Phase 2 (FR-2.4): the go/no-go artifact and the calibration reports state
# that they regenerate byte-identically from committed inputs, and a red-team
# verified it. That claim is only true if the checkout matches what the
# generator writes. The generators emit LF, so without this pin a Windows
# checkout holds CRLF and the reproducibility check fails on a file nobody
# touched — the same trap already pinned for data/ above, on the artifact that
# actually carries the phase verdict.
reports/phase2/*.md              text eol=lf
reports/phase2/*.json            text eol=lf
reports/phase2/*.txt             text eol=lf

# Recorded fixtures that tests hash or byte-compare.
tests/fixtures/**                text eol=lf

# ...but binary fixtures must never be EOL-normalized. `text eol=lf` above
# would strip CRLF byte pairs out of a GRIB2/Parquet/archive payload on
# check-in and hand the decoder a corrupt file. Workstream A hit exactly this
# during Phase 2 (9 byte pairs deleted from a 431,982-byte GRIB2 message) and
# base64-encoded its fixture to dodge it. Anything genuinely binary goes here.
tests/fixtures/**/*.grib2        -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.grb2         -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.parquet      -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.gz           -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.zip          -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.tgz          -text
tests/fixtures/**/*.png          -text
